#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135054 :tutorial/content "The **Gram-Schmidt process** is an algorithm that we can use to construct an *orthogonal* basis if we are given a non-orthogonal basis to start with.\n\nFor example, consider the basis $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ for the subspace $V=\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](images/c2/c2543117bc661286a5965534dfeeaaf6a066281ee58e2f39ea35143971d6837f.svg)\n\nWe can use the above basis to construct an *orthogonal* basis for the given $2$-dimensional subspace. To do this, we use the Gram-Schmidt process, which involves $2$ steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Set ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}=\\mathbf{a}_1.$\n\n- **Step 2:** Find a vector $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ in $V$ that's orthogonal to $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1.$ This can be achieved by computing\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/67/678cc056e3c9d3fbf17423ac591b3e85d84ee8060c0637840fe1c4c9e87aa878.png)\n\nNotice that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ is written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1(={\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1})$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So this means that ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\in V,$ and also ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\perp {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}.$\nMoreover, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\} = V = \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\}.\n$$\n\nAfter applying the Gram-Schmidt process, we get the set $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}\\},$ which is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nSo, in our example, we start by setting ![](images/72/7221215504f7dc7a831501ee23c0563434dd414ac05c1ef7e5761c2719e7d24d.svg) Then, we find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ that's orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 1+6\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/59/5944c42ffd5e609dcec914718180be9652a817de6b8cf84439d57cb106f03353.svg) is an orthogonal basis for $V.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b833ab7-f6d4-5c93-8709-37ecf29bf023" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3631 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135055 :tutorial/content "Given a basis $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ for a subspace $V,$ we can use the Gram-Schmidt process to find an *orthonormal* basis of the subspace.\n\nTo do this, we simply execute the Gram-Schmidt process as before to obtain an orthogonal basis $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2\\},$ and then we normalize each vector in this basis to get the orthonormal basis $\\{\\mathbf{u}_1, \\mathbf{u}_2\\},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_1}{\\Vert \\mathbf{v}_1 \\Vert},\n\t\\quad\n\t\\mathbf{u}_2 = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_2}{\\Vert \\mathbf{v}_2 \\Vert}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1ea8b19b-f7a8-5339-abd4-3069e18b2555" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3632 :tutorial/title "Using the Gram-Schmidt Process to Find an Orthonormal Basis"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135089 :tutorial/content "Sometimes it's helpful to \"clean up\" the result of a Gram-Schmidt process by scaling the resulting vectors.\n\nFor example, suppose that the set $\\mathcal B,$ given by\n\n![](images/79/79d1a4963f0e8a2e47dda0acf5c6031571574b1631d57c15d757f94b4f25468b.svg)\n\nis an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}.$\n\nSince the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ lies in $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2,$ the vector $3\\mathbf{v}_2$ also lies in $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp 3\\mathbf{v}_2.$ Therefore, taking \n\n![](images/c6/c69d0c835aa55e7e835c81a4338d56bd3fda97ebb8a73ed370e4384b80d23093.svg)\n\ninstead of $\\mathbf{v}_2,$ we again obtain an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/fc/fcdac688252b50d74ed8c4bddba578f027f3bfec5a356ec3fe54839638c1a0ae.svg)\n\nBut this time, the second vector in the basis looks slightly nicer and is probably easier to work with." :tutorial/id #uuid "00f219fa-5ae3-5e1d-acd0-7b7df752a433" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4351 :tutorial/title "Scaling the Vectors After the Gram-Schmidt Process"}]}
